.TH SEND-SECURE 1 "September 2025" "MyWallet Toolkit 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
send-secure \- production-hardened Bitcoin transaction sender
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B send-secure.sh
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR network ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR fee_rate ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR 0|1 ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR label ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.I from_address
.I to_address
.I amount_sat
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B send-secure.sh
is a production-hardened Bitcoin transaction sender that implements comprehensive security measures including defense-in-depth protection, input validation, and secure private key handling. It requires authentication through
.BR keyauth-secure (1)
and never exposes private keys in any context.

The transaction sender validates all inputs, checks balances, estimates fees, creates transactions, and provides dry-run capabilities for testing. All operations are secured with multiple layers of protection against various attack vectors.

.B CRITICAL SECURITY REQUIREMENT:
This command must be run through keyauth-secure.sh for secure private key access. Private keys are never passed as command line arguments.

.B PRODUCTION SECURITY FEATURES:
.IP \(bu 4
Defense in depth with multiple security layers
.IP \(bu 4
Comprehensive input validation and sanitization
.IP \(bu 4
Race condition prevention with atomic operations
.IP \(bu 4
Memory protection and secure cleanup
.IP \(bu 4
API request validation and response sanitization
.IP \(bu 4
Buffer overflow prevention with strict limits
.IP \(bu 4
Protection against command injection attacks

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet mode. Suppress warnings and progress output.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose mode. Show transaction details and processing steps.
.TP
.B \-n \fInetwork\fR
Network: mainnet or testnet. Auto-detected from network.sh if not specified.
.TP
.B \-f \fIfee_rate\fR
Fee rate in satoshis per byte (1-1000, default: 10).
.TP
.B \-d
Dry run mode. Create transaction but don't broadcast. Automatically disables broadcasting.
.TP
.B \-b \fI0|1\fR
Broadcast transaction: 1=yes, 0=no (default: 1, automatically disabled in dry-run mode).
.TP
.B \-l \fIlabel\fR
Key label for keyring access (default: "default", alphanumeric characters only).
.TP
.B \-h
Show help message and exit.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I from_address
Source Bitcoin address. Must match the private key stored in the secure keyring. Undergoes format validation and network compatibility checking.
.TP
.I to_address
Destination Bitcoin address. Validated for format correctness and network compatibility. Cannot be the same as from_address.
.TP
.I amount_sat
Amount to send in satoshis. Must be at least 546 (dust limit) and not exceed 21M BTC equivalent. Validated against available balance.

.SH SECURE USAGE
.SS Method 1: One-Command Authentication (Recommended)
.B keyauth-secure.sh send-secure.sh
.I from_address to_address amount_sat

This method provides the highest security by authenticating and executing in a single operation.

.SS Method 2: Separate Authentication
.nf
.B keyauth-secure.sh -t 120
.I # Authenticate for 2 minutes
.B send-secure.sh
.I from_address to_address amount_sat
.I # Execute with cached authentication
.fi

.SH VALIDATION AND SECURITY CHECKS
.SS Address Validation
All Bitcoin addresses undergo comprehensive validation:
.IP \(bu 4
Length limits (maximum 100 characters)
.IP \(bu 4
Format pattern matching for different address types
.IP \(bu 4
Network compatibility verification (mainnet vs testnet)
.IP \(bu 4
Self-send prevention

.SS Amount Validation
Transaction amounts are strictly validated:
.IP \(bu 4
Minimum amount: 546 satoshis (dust limit)
.IP \(bu 4
Maximum amount: 21M BTC in satoshis
.IP \(bu 4
Integer format validation
.IP \(bu 4
Balance sufficiency checking

.SS Fee Validation
Fee rates undergo bounds checking:
.IP \(bu 4
Minimum: 1 satoshi per byte
.IP \(bu 4
Maximum: 1000 satoshis per byte
.IP \(bu 4
Sanity checking against transaction amount
.IP \(bu 4
Fee estimation with proper size calculation

.SH TRANSACTION WORKFLOW
.IP 1. 4
Verify secure authentication (keyauth-secure.sh required)
.IP 2. 4
Validate all input arguments and options
.IP 3. 4
Auto-detect network if not specified
.IP 4. 4
Retrieve private key from secure keyring
.IP 5. 4
Verify address ownership by deriving address from private key
.IP 6. 4
Check current balance and UTXO availability
.IP 7. 4
Estimate transaction fee based on inputs/outputs
.IP 8. 4
Verify sufficient balance for amount + fee
.IP 9. 4
Create raw transaction (educational placeholder)
.IP 10. 4
Broadcast or display transaction (based on options)
.IP 11. 4
Perform secure cleanup of sensitive data

.SH API INTEGRATION
The transaction sender integrates securely with external APIs:
.SS Supported APIs
.IP \(bu 4
Blockstream.info (primary)
.IP \(bu 4
BlockCypher (secondary)

.SS API Security
.IP \(bu 4
URL validation and domain allowlisting
.IP \(bu 4
Response size limits (1MB maximum)
.IP \(bu 4
Timeout controls (30 seconds maximum)
.IP \(bu 4
JSON format validation
.IP \(bu 4
Error handling and sanitization

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Secure testnet transaction with dry-run:
.B keyauth-secure.sh send-secure.sh -n testnet -d mfrom... mto... 50000
.TP
Custom fee rate with verbose output:
.B keyauth-secure.sh send-secure.sh -v -f 25 from_addr to_addr 100000
.TP
Production transaction (mainnet):
.B keyauth-secure.sh send-secure.sh from_addr to_addr 25000
.TP
Two-step authentication process:
.nf
.B keyauth-secure.sh -t 300
.I # Authenticate for 5 minutes
.B send-secure.sh -d from_addr to_addr 10000
.I # Test with dry-run first
.B send-secure.sh from_addr to_addr 10000
.I # Execute actual transaction
.fi

.SH EDUCATIONAL MODE
.B IMPORTANT:
The current implementation includes educational placeholders for transaction creation and broadcasting. In production, this would require:
.IP \(bu 4
Proper UTXO parsing and coin selection algorithms
.IP \(bu 4
Complete transaction structure creation
.IP \(bu 4
secp256k1 signature generation
.IP \(bu 4
Transaction serialization to hex format
.IP \(bu 4
Broadcasting to Bitcoin network nodes

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Transaction created successfully (may not be broadcast in educational mode)
.TP
.B 1
Authentication required, validation error, insufficient funds, or system error

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BITCOIN_KEYRING_ACTIVE
Must be set to "1" by keyauth-secure.sh to indicate authenticated context
.TP
.B BITCOIN_KEYRING_LABEL
Key label for keyring access (set by keyauth-secure.sh)
.TP
.B BITCOIN_SESSION_ID
Session identifier for secure operations

